      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grangerland%2C_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Oil was discovered in Grangerland in 1931, right in the middle of Texas's great oil boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grangerland%2C_Texas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Edwin Granger built thirty‑two houses to house the influx of oil‑field workers in the early 1930s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/grangerland-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The cluster of homes called Granger's Camp grew into the community known today as Grangerland during the 1940s and 1950s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grangerland%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Grangerland sits on Farm Road 3083, roughly thirty miles north of Houston and ten miles southeast of Conroe.",
